# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
./filetug

# AI
.claude/settings.local.json

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
.output*.txt
*.out
coverage.*
coverage*.out
coverage*.html
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

cpuprofile
**/mem.prof
**/cpu.prof

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/
.idea
specscore.local.yaml
**/.*.lifecycle-transaction.lock

# GoReleaser writes ./dist during a release. Go's VCS stamping counts UNTRACKED
# files as a dirty tree, so leaving it unignored makes every released binary
# report a `+dirty` version: `go version -m` on the published artifact shows
# e.g. `v0.13.3+dirty`. GoReleaser's own dirty check only looks at tracked
# modifications, so it never complains, and the version string lies quietly.
dist/
